K-Means Clustering: 
K-Means Clustering is one of the most popular and widely used unsupervised learning algorithms. It is a centroid-based clustering 
algorithm that partitions data into K clusters based on their similarity. The goal of K-Means is to group data points such that the points in 
the same cluster are as similar as possible, and the points in different clusters are as dissimilar as possible. 
 K-Means is a partitioning method that divides data into k clusters. Each cluster is defined by its centroid, and data points are 
assigned to the nearest centroid. 
 Steps: 
o Randomly initialize k centroids. 
o Assign each data point to the nearest centroid. 
o  
o Recompute the centroids as the mean of all points assigned to them. 
o Repeat the assignment and update steps until convergence. 
The K-Means Algorithm  
1. Select K: Choose the number of clusters, K. 
2. Randomly initialize centroids: Choose K data points as initial centroids. 
3. Assign each data point to the nearest centroid: Using Euclidean distance, assign each data point to the nearest centroid. 
4. Recalculate centroids: For each cluster, compute the mean of the data points in that cluster to update the centroid. 
5. Repeat: Reassign the data points to the new centroids and update the centroids, until convergence (when centroids stop changing). 
